    "dynamic_faq_8AUh7M": {
      "type": "dynamic-faq",
      "blocks": {
        "question_nCTWbR": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Is the Grip Tread Tire Edition autonomous, like a robot mower?",
            "answer": "<p>No. It's a remote-controlled mower, not a self-navigating robot. You operate it with a handheld controller from a safe distance. It doesn't map your yard, use GPS, or run scheduled mows on its own.<br/><br/></p>",
            "icon": "none"
          }
        },
        "question_69jhiF": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What makes this different from the standard S1 4WD or the Wide Wheels Edition?",
            "answer": "<p>The tires and slope rating. The standard 4WD is rated for 75% grade (37°), the Wide Wheels Edition for 85% (40°), and the Grip Tread Tire Edition for 100% grade (45°), the highest in the S1 lineup. If your yard has genuinely steep sections, this is the configuration built for it.<br/><br/></p>",
            "icon": "none"
          }
        },
        "question_8KCdGW": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How steep is a 100% grade, really?",
            "answer": "<p>A 100% grade is a 45 degree slope, steep enough that walking a traditional push mower up it isn't realistic or safe. That's the terrain this configuration is built for. Always mow across a slope rather than straight up or down it.<br/><br/></p>",
            "icon": "none"
          }
        },
        "question_EcA6Mx": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can it handle wet or loose ground on a slope?",
            "answer": "<p>That's the point of the Grip Tread tires. They deform and flex to maintain contact on loose soil, mud, and wet grass, where standard tires would lose traction and slip. Extremely saturated or muddy ground is still best avoided regardless of tire type.<br/><br/></p>",
            "icon": "none"
          }
        },
        "question_BQYmLz": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Does the 18Ah battery cover a full mow on hilly terrain?",
            "answer": "<p>You get up to 2.25 hours of runtime and up to 1.125 acres per charge under typical conditions. Steep, continuous climbing draws more power than flat ground, so actual coverage on a heavily sloped property may run toward the lower end of that range.<br/><br/></p>",
            "icon": "none"
          }
        },
        "question_JjXiyk": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Who can operate it safely on a slope?",
            "answer": "<p>The remote is simple to use, but operating on steep terrain still requires attention. Stand at a stable, safe distance, keep children and pets clear of the mowing area, and never operate it from a position where you could lose your footing.<br/><br/></p>",
            "icon": "none"
          }
        },
        "question_JxkamV": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Does it require different maintenance because of the tires?",
            "answer": "<p>Basic upkeep is the same as any S1: keep the blade clean and sharp, clear debris from the deck after use, and store the battery partially charged if it'll sit unused. Inspect the Grip Tread tires periodically for embedded debris or damage, since they see more contact stress on rough terrain.<br/><br/></p>",
            "icon": "none"
          }
        }
      },
